IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `va-vandalism`

-- Vandalism Act 1966 -- the questions of the person accused of vandalism.
--
-- SEAT: the person who did, tried to do, or caused someone else to do the act -- the one the Act is applied to.
-- This layer phrases the rules in va-vandalism.l4 as that person's questions and changes none of them. Each question is
-- published with @export so that it can be deployed to Legalese Cloud and asked from a form.
--
-- Defaults (ruled 2026-10-18): the Act alone; the actors it names; judgments handed to others asked as facts (the
-- officer's reasonable belief, s 5; the Criminal Procedure Code exclusions from caning); the Act's definitions worked
-- out (act of vandalism, public property, s 4(1) person); one act is one case. Departures in the rules underneath:
-- ss 7-8 (stolen public property, dealers' licences) address a receiver and the court, not the vandal, so only the s 7
-- presumption is asked here; s 8 is left out.

§ `Is it vandalism, and is it an offence`

-- s 2: an "act of vandalism" is (a) writing, drawing, marking, posting up a notice or hanging a flag or banner on
-- public property, or on private property without the owner's written permission, or (b) stealing, destroying or
-- damaging public property. "Public property" is property of the Government, a statutory body, or an armed force
-- lawfully present in Singapore.
@export Is what I did an act of vandalism under s 2?
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A Conduct @desc What you did
      `the owner` IS AN Owner @desc Who owns the property
      `had written permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the owner had given you written permission
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is what I did vandalism` IF
    `is an act of vandalism` `the conduct` `the owner` `had written permission`

-- s 3: "Any person who commits or attempts to commit or abets the commission of any act of vandalism" is guilty of an
-- offence. Abetting is read as causing it to be done (the encoding's input).
@export Have I committed the s 3 offence of vandalism?
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A Conduct @desc What you did
      `the owner` IS AN Owner @desc Who owns the property
      `had written permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the owner had given you written permission
      `committed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did it yourself
      `attempted it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you tried to do it
      `caused it to be done` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you got someone else to do it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I committed a vandalism offence` IF
        `is an act of vandalism` `the conduct` `the owner` `had written permission`
    AND `is a section 3 offence` `committed it` `attempted it` `caused it to be done`

§ `What can happen to me`

-- s 3: "a fine not exceeding $2,000 or ... imprisonment for a term not exceeding 3 years".
@export What is the most I can be fined for vandalism (s 3), in dollars?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined` IS `maximum fine in dollars`

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned for vandalism (s 3), in months?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the longest I can be imprisoned in months` IS `maximum imprisonment in months`

-- s 3: "shall also be punished with caning with not less than 3 strokes and not more than 8 strokes", except on a first
-- conviction for writing or marking with a delible substance, or for posting a notice or hanging a flag or banner.
-- The Criminal Procedure Code exclusions (ss 325(1), 330(1): for example, women and men over 50) are asked as a fact.
@export Must I be caned if I am convicted of vandalism (s 3)?
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A Conduct @desc What you did
      `first conviction under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this would be your first conviction under the Vandalism Act
      `done with a delible substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For writing or marking: whether it was done with something that can be removed (a delible substance)
      `excluded by CPC ss 325(1) or 330(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Criminal Procedure Code excludes you from caning (for example, because you are a woman or a man over 50)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I be caned` IF
    `caning must be imposed` `the conduct` `first conviction under this Act` `done with a delible substance` `excluded by CPC ss 325(1) or 330(1)`

@export If I am caned for vandalism (s 3), what is the fewest strokes?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the fewest strokes of the cane` IS `minimum strokes of the cane`

@export If I am caned for vandalism (s 3), what is the most strokes?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most strokes of the cane` IS `maximum strokes of the cane`

-- s 6: the offences under the Act are "arrestable" (arrest without warrant) and "non-bailable" (bail is not a right).
@export Can I be arrested without a warrant, and refused bail as of right (s 6)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence @desc The offence you are suspected of
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be arrested without a warrant and refused bail` IF
    `is arrestable and non-bailable under this Act` `the offence`

§ `If I am stopped`

-- s 4(1): a police officer (and the other officers listed) may demand that a person produce an article believed to be
-- connected with an offence under the Act; s 4(2): refusing is an offence, with a fine of up to $500.
@export Do I have to hand over the article when this person asks (s 4(1))?
GIVEN `the demander` IS A Demander @desc Who is asking you to hand it over
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I have to hand it over` IF
    `is a section 4(1) person` `the demander`

@export Have I committed an offence by not handing it over (s 4(2))?
GIVEN `the demander` IS A Demander @desc Who asked you to hand it over
      `produced it on demand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you handed it over when asked
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it an offence that I did not hand it over` IF
    `failing to produce is an offence` `the demander` `produced it on demand`

@export What is the most I can be fined for not handing it over (s 4(2)), in dollars?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined for not handing it over` IS `maximum fine for failing to produce in dollars`

-- s 5: a s 4(1) person may seize an article "which he reasonably believes" relates to an offence or is evidence.
-- The belief is the officer's judgment, asked as a fact.
@export Can this person take the article from me (s 5)?
GIVEN `the demander` IS A Demander @desc Who wants to take the article
      `reasonably believes it relates to an offence or is evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that person reasonably believes it relates to an offence or is evidence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can they take the article from me` IF
    `may seize the article` `the demander` `reasonably believes it relates to an offence or is evidence`
